<div class="card mb-4 border-dark">
  <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">Verbal Instructions (Parts A and B)</div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <button class="btn btn-outline-light btn-sm mb-2" onclick="toggleSection('inst_vi')">Show Instructions</button>
    <div id="inst_vi" class="collapsible-section mb-3">
      <p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Evaluates auditory processing, sequential memory, and the ability to execute complex verbal instructions sensitive to attention span, language comprehension, and cognitive load. Highlights working memory capacity and the impact of distraction or divided attention. Mimics real-world classroom environments where verbal information volume is high and working memory demands are elevated.</p>

      <p><strong>Administration:</strong> 
      Examiner gives a series of increasingly complex commands (one through five steps). Instructions should be delivered clearly, at natural conversation pace, and not repeated unless formally trialing accommodations post-task. Materials: Page two of response book (dots, squares, x’s, lines, circles of varying size and thickness).</p>

      <p><strong>Script:</strong> “I'm going to ask you to do some things on this page. Notice that there are dots at the top. Then there are three squares, a large one, a middle-sized one, and a small one. There are a big X, a middle-sized X, and two small X's. Then a short line and a long line, a thick line and a thin line, and two big circles and two small circles. I'm going to give you some instructions to follow. I'm only allowed to say these instructions once, so please listen very carefully.”</p>

      <p><strong>Accommodations (optional after full task):</strong> Slower reading pace, chunking instructions into parts, allowing child to repeat instructions aloud before acting.</p>

      <p><strong>Scoring:</strong> Each item scored individually. 1 = correct execution, 0 = incorrect or minor errors. Specific mistakes should be noted for clinical interpretation.</p>

      <p><strong>Clinical Interpretation Overview:</strong> Part A uses simple syntax with low working memory load. Part B introduces dual-step or non-sequential commands, requiring greater working memory, sequencing, and auditory rehearsal. Performance helps differentiate pure memory weaknesses from higher-order executive dysfunction.</p>
    </div>

    <h5>Part A: Condensed Syntax</h5>

    <label for="vi_a1">Item 1: Put your thumb in a big circle and then use your pencil to touch the other one. (Cognitive Demand: Visual tracking and dual step)</label>
